<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miroslav Krleža</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1893–1981</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miroslav Krleža was a Croatian writer and the leading figure of twentieth-century Croatian literature. His novels, plays and essays, including The Return of Philip Latinowicz (1932) and the Glembay cycle, criticised bourgeois society and nationalism. A left-wing intellectual who defended artistic freedom against Stalinist dogma, he later headed the Yugoslav Lexicographical Institute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miroslav-krleza.jpg" alt="Miroslav Krleža" width="193" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miroslav_Krle%C5%BEa_1953.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
